    content: "---\nname: diagnosing-bugs\ndescription: Isolate root causes from stack traces, native crashes, and logic anomalies via minimal reproduction before patching.\nwhen_not_to_use: Greenfield feature design, PR style reviews, or documentation.\nverified: \"2026-11\"\ntriggers: [stacktrace, reproduce bug, root cause, memory leak, race condition]\n---\n# Diagnosing Bugs (v3)\n\n## 1. Failure Signature -> Triage Matrix\n- **JVM / Kotlin Exception**: Skip wrapper frames; read the deepest `Caused by:` line and exact file:line mutation point.\n- **Native Crash (`SIGSEGV` / `SIGABRT` / `Fatal signal`)**: Inspect JNI/FFI boundary (`lib*.so`), null pointers, UTF-8 conversion, or Rust `.unwrap()`/`expect()` panics.\n- **Race Condition / Heisenbug**: Audit shared mutable state across `Dispatchers.Main` vs `IO/Default`, uncancelled coroutines, or stale UI closures.\n- **Silent Wrong Output**: Log raw inputs and outputs at module boundaries before touching internal transforms.\n\n## 2. Execution Protocol\n1. **Reproduce First**: Run the failing test, build command, or minimal CLI reproducer to capture the exact failure signal.\n2. **Trace Backward**: Pinpoint the single state transition where expected invariant diverges from actual value.\n3. **Surgical Patch**: Fix the root cause with the smallest possible diff; never mask errors with empty `try/catch` blocks.\n\n## 3. Hard Invariants & Verification Gate\n- **NEVER** edit code before proving the root cause or confirming a reproducible failure signal.\n- **Verification**: Re-run the exact reproducer command (must pass) and check `git diff` to confirm zero unrelated edits.\n",
